# Cursor AI Rules for Steam Config Analyzer

## Project Context
- This is a Steam configuration management tool with CLI and planned GUI
- Uses Python backend with VDF parsing for Steam configuration files
- Target platform: Steam Deck and Linux systems
- Focus: User-friendly interface for non-technical users

## Development Workflow
1. **Backend First**: Test CLI functionality and core logic
2. **Safety First**: Always test with Steam closed, create backups
3. **Modular Development**: Use the new modular architecture
4. **Documentation**: Update hub-and-spoke documentation as you go

## CRITICAL: Steam Safety Rules
- **MANDATORY**: Steam must be closed before any configuration updates
- **MANDATORY**: Test all changes with real Steam configuration files
- **MANDATORY**: Verify automatic backups are created before changes
- **MANDATORY**: Test rollback functionality with backup files
- **NEVER**: Modify Steam-managed fields (cloud, autocloud, BadgeData, EULA)
- **ALWAYS**: Validate field safety before updates
- **ALWAYS**: Test with actual Steam games, not mock data

## Code Quality Standards
- Follow the modular architecture (cli.py, core/, api/, utils/)
- Use type hints and docstrings for all public methods
- Test all CLI commands before committing
- Maintain backward compatibility with existing JSON files
- Use the hub-and-spoke documentation pattern

## Testing Requirements
- **MANDATORY**: Test CLI commands with real Steam data
- **MANDATORY**: Test both sparse and standard extraction modes
- **MANDATORY**: Test update operations with Steam closed
- **MANDATORY**: Verify backup creation and restore functionality
- **MANDATORY**: Test field validation and safety checks
- Test with different Steam user IDs if applicable

## File Structure Rules
- Backend code goes in `backend/src/`
- User data goes in `backend/instance/` (gitignored)
- Documentation follows hub-and-spoke pattern
- Admin planning in `admin/planning/`
- Tests in `tests/` with proper structure

## Documentation Standards
- Use hub-and-spoke pattern for all documentation
- Update README.md files as entry points
- Keep documents focused (one purpose per document)
- Link between related documents
- Update status and checkboxes as work progresses

## Git Flow Rules
- Use feature branches: `feat/feature-name`
- Use fix branches: `fix/issue-description`
- Use docs branches: `docs/documentation-update`
- Use chore branches: `chore/maintenance-task`
- All code changes require PRs
- Documentation can be pushed directly to develop/main

## Steam Deck Considerations
- Test on Steam Deck Desktop Mode
- Ensure touch-friendly interface for future GUI
- Consider controller navigation for GUI
- Optimize for Steam Deck performance
- Test with actual Steam Deck games

## Current Focus Areas
- Complete project restructuring (in progress)
- Modular architecture implementation
- Comprehensive testing infrastructure
- GUI framework selection and development
- User experience optimization for Steam Deck

## Safety Checklist Before Commits
- [ ] Steam is closed during testing
- [ ] Automatic backups are working
- [ ] Field validation is working
- [ ] CLI commands work with real data
- [ ] Documentation is updated
- [ ] Tests pass (when available)
- [ ] No Steam-managed fields are modified
- [ ] Rollback functionality works